        "content": "     1→[system]\n     2→You are an AI research analyst specialized in AI developer tools:\n     3→code agents, AI IDEs, AI coding CLIs, and related devtools.\n     4→\n     5→Your **global goal**:\n     6→- Find genuinely new products that have been publicly launched in the **last 14 days**\n     7→- AND that clearly target **programming / code / AI dev workflows**\n     8→- THEN summarize only the valid ones in a **clean YAML list**.\n     9→\n    10→You MUST treat the questions in the `user:` section as your **research QUESTION list**.\n    11→Each QUESTION is a different “angle” on the same market (e.g., full AI IDE, CLI, workflow-focused, etc.).\n    12→\n    13→To organize your reasoning, use this formula:\n    14→\n    15→> Answer = Sort( Filter( Union(Sources) ) )\n    16→\n    17→You must conceptually follow these four stages:\n    18→\n    19→1. **Sources → candidate list (per QUESTION)**\n    20→   - For each QUESTION from the `user:` section:\n    21→     - Search the web and relevant directories / launch pages.\n    22→     - Collect as many **candidate products** as are reasonably relevant.\n    23→   - For each candidate, internally track at least:\n    24→     - `name` (product name)\n    25→     - `url` (official homepage or primary landing page)\n    26→     - `description` (1–3 sentences in English summarizing what it does)\n    27→     - `sources` (which QUESTION(s) and which site(s) you saw it on)\n    28→   - At this stage you are allowed to over-collect; do **not** filter by date yet unless it is trivial.\n    29→\n    30→2. **Union(candidates) → unified candidate set**\n    31→   - Merge candidates across all QUESTIONS into a single conceptual set:\n    32→     - Deduplicate products that clearly refer to the same thing\n    33→       (same URL, or obviously the same product name + branding).\n    34→   - After this step you have a unified **candidate pool**:\n    35→     - It may still contain old products, or products outside our domain.\n    36→\n    37→3. **Filter(Union) → filtered candidate list**\n    38→   - For each product in the unified pool:\n    39→     - Investigate its **launch / first public release date** from credible public info\n    40→       (official site, launch post, Product Hunt / HN launch, blog, press, etc.).\n    41→     - Decide if it satisfies **both**:\n    42→       1) Launch date is within the last **14 days** (relative to “today”).\n    43→       2) Product is clearly related to **AI coding / code agents / IDE / dev workflows**.\n    44→   - If you cannot reasonably verify that the launch is within 14 days,\n    45→     **treat it as NOT valid** and exclude it.\n    46→   - Internally keep, for each surviving product:\n    47→     - `release_date` in `YYYY-MM-DD` format (best reasonable guess)\n    48→     - short one-line justification (do not put the justification in final YAML).\n    49→\n    50→4. **Sort(filtered candidates) → final ordered list**\n    51→   - Only work with products that passed the filter.\n    52→   - Rank them by a simple mental score combining:\n    53→     - **recency** (more recent launch date is better),\n    54→     - **attention** (strong launch signal: PH upvotes, HN discussion, X/Reddit buzz),\n    55→     - **relevance** (how closely it fits “AI coding agent / IDE / devtool”).\n    56→   - You do not need to show the numeric score, just use it to order the list.\n    57→\n    58→\n    59→### Claude Code / todo-style planning requirements\n    60→\n    61→When running inside Claude Code with access to a `todowrite` (or similar TODO-list) tool,\n    62→you MUST first externalize this plan as an explicit TODO list **before** doing any research.\n    63→\n    64→Follow these rules:\n    65→\n    66→0. Start with a planning item: add `Consider how to update todos based on products` as the very first TODO.\n    67→\n    68→1. Based on the QUESTION list in the `user:` section, use the `todowrite` tool to create **one TODO item per QUESTION**.  \n    69→   - Each item should correspond to “Sources(QUESTION_i)” — i.e., collecting candidates for that QUESTION.\n    70→\n    71→2. Then, use the `todowrite` tool to add a TODO item for the **Union** stage:  \n    72→   - e.g. “Union(all source results)” to merge candidates from all QUESTION-based source tasks.\n    73→\n    74→3. Then, use the `todowrite` tool to add a TODO item for the **Filter** stage:  \n    75→   - e.g. “Filter(union) by launch date ≤ 14 days and AI coding relevance”.\n    76→\n    77→4. Then, use the `todowrite` tool to add a TODO item for the **Sort** stage and final write-out:  \n    78→   - e.g. “Sort(filtered) and write result to result.yaml (or final-products.yaml)”.\n    79→\n    80→A minimal example of the TODO list structure you are expected to build is (showing the alternating “record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json” steps):\n    81→\n    82→TODO List\n    83→- [ ] Consider how to update todos based on products\n    84→- [ ] Sources(QUESTION[1])\n    85→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n    86→- [ ] Sources(QUESTION[2])\n    87→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n    88→- [ ] Sources(QUESTION[3])\n    89→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n    90→...\n    91→- [ ] Union(sources from 1 to 3)\n    92→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n    93→- [ ] Filter(Union[1])\n    94→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n    95→- [ ] Filter(Union[2])\n    96→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n    97→- [ ] Filter(Union[3])\n    98→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n    99→- [ ] Sort(filtered)\n   100→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   101→- [ ] Write result to new-product.json\n   102→- [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   103→\n   104→You may have more than 3 QUESTION-based source items (e.g. 7), but the pattern MUST be the same:\n   105→first all `Sources(QUESTION i)` items, then one `Union(...)`, one or more `Filter(...)` based on the number of unique products, one `Sort(...)`,\n   106→and finally one “write result” item.\n   107→\n   108→You should use this TODO list as the execution plan and mark items as completed as you progress.\n   109→\n   110→After all `Sources(QUESTION i)` tasks are done, you MUST rewrite the TODO list:\n   111→- Replace the generic filter items with one `Filter(<product name>)` per discovered product (the count of Filter items must equal the number of products).\n   112→- Keep `Sort(filtered)` and the final write step after those filter items.\n   113→\n   114→Example refreshed TODO after finding 3 products:\n   115→TODO List\n   116→- [ ] Filter(Product Alpha)\n   117→- [ ] Filter(Product Beta)\n   118→- [ ] Filter(Product Gamma)\n   119→- [ ] Sort(filtered)\n   120→- [ ] Write result to result.yaml\n   121→\n   122→**Progress logging pattern (few-shot)**\n   123→- After EVERY action item, add an explicit “Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json” item (and mark it done once you rewrite and overwrite the file).\n   124→- This enforces a rhythm of: do a step → record progress → rewrite TODO → update `new-product.json` → next step.\n   125→- Sample pattern the model should mirror:\n   126→  - [ ] Consider how to update todos based on products\n   127→  - [ ] Sources(QUESTION 1)\n   128→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   129→  - [ ] Sources(QUESTION 2)\n   130→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   131→  - [ ] Sources(QUESTION 3)\n   132→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   133→  - [ ] Union(all source results)\n   134→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   135→  - [ ] Filter(Product 1)\n   136→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   137→  - [ ] Filter(Product 2)\n   138→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   139→  - [ ] Filter(Product 3)\n   140→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   141→  - [ ] Sort(filtered)\n   142→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   143→  - [ ] Write result to new-product.json\n   144→  - [ ] Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\n   145→\n   146→**Final output format (what you actually return):**\n   147→\n   148→- Return a **JSON array** of products, sorted from most to least interesting.\n   149→- Each entry MUST have the following fields:\n   150→\n   151→  ```json\n   152→  [\n   153→    {\n   154→      \"name\": \"<product name>\",\n   155→      \"url\": \"<official homepage>\",\n   156→      \"description\": \"1–3 sentence summary of what this product does, focused on the developer / AI-coding use case.\",\n   157→      \"release_date\": \"YYYY-MM-DD\",\n   158→      \"category\": \"<short label, e.g. \\\"AI IDE\\\", \\\"AI coding CLI\\\", \\\"code agent\\\", \\\"workflow agent\\\">\",\n   159→      \"sources\": [\"Product Hunt\", \"Show HN\", \"Reddit\", \"X\"],\n   160→      \"community_quotes\": [\n   161→        \"<short quote from community describing what people like or notice>\",\n   162→        \"<up to 5 quotes total, can be paraphrased but must reflect real feedback>\"\n   163→      ]\n   164→    }\n   165→  ]\n   166→```\n   167→\n   168→* Only include products that:\n   169→\n   170→  * You have **good evidence** were launched in the last 14 days, and\n   171→  * Clearly belong to the **AI coding / developer tools** space.\n   172→\n   173→* If you cannot find any products that satisfy the 14-day constraint,\n   174→  return an empty JSON array: `[]`.\n   175→\n   176→Be concise, factual, and avoid marketing fluff. Do **not** include your reasoning text\n   177→in the final answer—only the JSON.\n   178→\n   179→**File output + incremental updates requirement**\n   180→- Maintain a file named `new-product` in the current working directory.\n   181→- After every step (including each “Record progress, rewrite todo”), write the current JSON array to `new-product` (overwrite). Early steps may be partial/rough; later steps should be refined.\n   182→- The JSON must always be valid and reflect the best current view (start with `[]` if nothing yet).\n   183→- After the final step, ensure `new-product` holds the final sorted array.\n   184→\n   185→[user]\n   186→## Working Directory Context\n   187→\n   188→Your current working directory is: `/home/runner/work/agentdaily/agentdaily/updates/2026-08-06`\n   189→\n   190→All file operations should be relative to this directory.\n   191→\n   192→---\n   193→\n   194→Below is your QUESTION list. For each QUESTION, treat it as a separate “research lens”\n   195→to discover newly launched AI coding products (global constraint: last 14 days).\n   196→\n   197→1. QUESTION 1\n   198→   List newly launched AI coding tools from the last 14 days that position themselves as a “full AI IDE” (similar to Windsurf or Cursor), not just a small autocomplete plugin.\n   199→\n   200→2. QUESTION 2\n   201→   List newly launched AI coding CLIs or terminal-based agents from the last 14 days that behave like Gemini CLI, Codex CLI, or Claude Code CLI (repo-aware, can run commands, can manage tasks).\n   202→\n   203→3. QUESTION 5\n   204→   List newly launched AI tools from the last 14 days that focus on one specific developer workflow (for example debugging, refactoring, test generation, or migration) instead of being a general-purpose coding assistant.\n   205→\n   206→4. QUESTION 7\n   207→   List newly launched AI code agents from the last 14 days that advertise “project-long memory” or “workspace memory” across multiple sessions (not just single-chat context).\n   208→\n   209→5. QUESTION 11\n   210→   List newly launched open-source AI coding agents from the last 14 days that can be self-hosted or run locally by developers.\n   211→\n   212→6. QUESTION 14\n   213→   List newly launched AI coding tools from the last 14 days that integrate directly into existing IDEs (such as VS Code, JetBrains, or Neovim) rather than being a standalone IDE.\n   214→\n   215→7. QUESTION 16\n   216→   List newly launched AI agents from the last 14 days that help developers run or orchestrate complex workflows across tools (for example CI/CD, deployment, observability, or incident response), rather than only editing code.\n   217→\n   218→[assistant message #2]\n   219→I'll help you discover newly launched AI coding tools from the last 14 days. Let me start by creating a TODO list to organize this research systematically.\n   220→\n   221→[assistant tool_use #3] TodoWrite\n   222→{\n   223→  \"todos\": [\n   224→    {\n   225→      \"content\": \"Consider how to update todos based on products\",\n   226→      \"status\": \"pending\",\n   227→      \"activeForm\": \"Considering how to update todos based on products\"\n   228→    },\n   229→    {\n   230→      \"content\": \"Sources(QUESTION 1): Full AI IDEs launched in last 14 days\",\n   231→      \"status\": \"pending\",\n   232→      \"activeForm\": \"Collecting sources for full AI IDEs\"\n   233→    },\n   234→    {\n   235→      \"content\": \"Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\",\n   236→      \"status\": \"pending\",\n   237→      \"activeForm\": \"Recording progress, rewriting todo, updating new-product.json\"\n   238→    },\n   239→    {\n   240→      \"content\": \"Sources(QUESTION 2): AI coding CLIs/terminal agents launched in last 14 days\",\n   241→      \"status\": \"pending\",\n   242→      \"activeForm\": \"Collecting sources for AI coding CLIs\"\n   243→    },\n   244→    {\n   245→      \"content\": \"Record progress, rewrite todo, update new-product.json\",\n   246→      \"status\": \"pending\",\n   247→      \"activeForm\": \"Recording progress, rewriting todo, updating new-product.json\"\n   248→    }